        explanation: "Every good story has three main parts, like a sandwich! 🥪\n\n📖 BEGINNING (Introduction):\n• Introduce the CHARACTERS (who is the story about?)\n• Set the SCENE (where and when does it happen?)\n• Hook the reader (make them want to read more!)\nExample: \"Once upon a time, in a small village near a magical forest, there lived a clever girl named Meera and her talking parrot, Mithu.\"\n\n⚡ MIDDLE (Problem/Adventure):\n• Something HAPPENS! A challenge, mystery, or adventure\n• This is the LONGEST part of the story\n• The character tries to solve the problem\n• Things might go wrong before they go right!\nExample: \"One day, Meera found a mysterious map hidden inside an old book. It showed the way to a treasure deep in the magical forest. But the forest was full of tricky puzzles and talking animals!\"\n\n🎉 ENDING (Resolution):\n• The problem is SOLVED\n• Characters learn something important (the MORAL)\n• Everything wraps up satisfactorily\nExample: \"With Mithu's help and her own cleverness, Meera solved every puzzle. The treasure turned out to be a library full of magical books! Meera shared them with the village, and everyone learned that the greatest treasure is knowledge.\"",
        example: "Famous story structures:\n\n🐢🐰 The Tortoise and the Hare:\nBeginning: A hare boasts he's the fastest. A tortoise challenges him.\nMiddle: The hare runs ahead, then naps. The tortoise keeps walking slowly.\nEnding: The tortoise wins! Moral: Slow and steady wins the race.\n\n🐦🐾 The Thirsty Crow:\nBeginning: A thirsty crow finds a pot with very little water.\nMiddle: The crow's beak can't reach! He thinks of a clever plan.\nEnding: He drops pebbles one by one, water rises, and he drinks! Moral: Where there's a will, there's a way.",
